  "solution": {
    "text": "For Refraction at first surface <math><mfrac><mrow><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow></mfrac></math><br /><br /><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi></math><br /><br /><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></math><br /><br />Therefore, rays strike the mirror normally. So, by principle of reversibility, <math><mtext>O&nbsp;</mtext></math>and<math><mtext>&nbsp;I</mtext></math>&nbsp;coincide.",
